    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d166-a1">

                <pb facs="0311" n="223" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg223"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 656.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Munn &amp; Co., to the
                            President</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >New York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-12-11"><hi rendition="#italic">December 11,
                        1891</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: We learn from your official message to the
                    Senate and House of Representatives, dated 9th instant, that international
                    copyright has been secured with Great Britain and the British Possessions, the
                    laws of those countries permitting to our citizens the benefit of copyright on
                    substantially the same basis as to their own citizens and subjects.</p>
                <p>We inclose an official letter from the Canadian registrar, dated August 22, 1891,
                    which denies the right of American citizens to obtain copyright protection in
                    Canada, and is to that extent contradictory of the message.</p>
                <p>The matter is one of considerable importance to our citizens who at present are
                    unable to obtain any protection in Canada, although registration is allowed to
                    Canadians in this country.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully, etc.,</p>
                <closer><hi rendition="#small-caps">Munn</hi> &amp; Co.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
